# arvo-strategy

A name bound to what you are optimising for.

A strategy here is a binding and not a switch. It maps a name onto a placement objective, which is
what the derivation in `arvo-placement` is keyed on, and onto which adaptation gets selected per
operation. It does not name an implementation and it does not own one.

The set is open. Four names come with the crate, fast, compact, accurate, and a compromise meant to be
the sensible default, but they are implementors of a concept rather than members of an enumeration,
and adding a fifth needs no edit to anything already here.

What each of those four is for is written down as an intent, deliberately, and not as a table of
behaviours. The intents are clear enough to build against and nothing about them is absolute, so a
document stating them as settled rules would be claiming more than is known. The fast one may trade
soundness away, that is its whole purpose, but not for nothing, the gain has to be provable. The
compact one keeps memory and disk small and has room to be slow, though it does not have to take it
and mostly should not. The accurate one gives up speed to be right, especially across a chain rather
than one operation at a time. The compromise aims at being the intuitive choice for most things, and
being a Rust crate, how Rust does it is where intuition starts, but that is a starting point and not a
rule, and it gets dropped where following it is just the worse answer.

All of them decide by measurement. They measure different things and they weigh what they measure
differently, and that difference is the whole reason there is more than one of them.

There is no exchange rate between the objectives, on purpose. Nothing here says how much speed a unit
of accuracy is worth, because a rate like that is a generalisation over a whole category and this
library is built the other way around, out of many small pieces that each hold where they hold and
nowhere else. Where two objectives genuinely conflict, whatever resolves it says in words why, and
that reason gets written down beside it.

`no_std`, no allocation, and the selection happens at compile time. Nothing here branches at runtime.

## Status

Design phase. The documents are written and the source is being built from them.
